Yes, this issue exists across all Java versions. Apologies for referencing an outdated document


https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/21/docs/api/java.base/java/lang/Object.html#wait()

OpenJDK has recommended the following solution.



API Note:
The recommended approach to waiting is to check the condition being awaited in a while loop around the call to wait, as shown in the example below. Among other things, this approach avoids problems that can be caused by spurious wakeups.

     synchronized (obj) {
         while (<condition does not hold> and <timeout not exceeded>) {
             long timeoutMillis = ... ; // recompute timeout values
             int nanos = ... ;
             obj.wait(timeoutMillis, nanos);
         }
         ... // Perform action appropriate to condition or timeout
     }

