Root cause (verified against current trunk)

The crash signature in this ticket (e.g. Received request or response with leader OptionalInt[3] and epoch 55 which is inconsistent with current leader OptionalInt.empty) comes from KafkaRaftClient.maybeTransition:


private void maybeTransition(
    OptionalInt leaderId,
    int epoch,
    Endpoints leaderEndpoints,
    long currentTimeMs
) {
    if (!hasConsistentLeader(epoch, leaderId)) {
        throw new IllegalStateException("Received request or response with leader "  leaderId 
            " and epoch "  epoch  " which is inconsistent with current leader " +
            quorum.leaderId()  " and epoch "  quorum.epoch());
    }
    ...
}


and hasConsistentLeader:


private boolean hasConsistentLeader(int epoch, OptionalInt leaderId) {
    if (leaderId.isPresent() && leaderId.getAsInt() == quorum.localIdOrSentinel()) {
        // The response indicates that we should be the leader, so we verify that is the case
        return quorum.isLeader();
    } else {
        ...
    }
}


So whenever an inbound request or response asserts "the leader is you" for a node that does not currently believe it is the leader, this throws unconditionally.

Why this is fatal, not just logged

maybeTransition is invoked from the raft I/O thread. Any exception it throws propagates out of KafkaRaftClient.poll() to KafkaRaftClientDriver.doWork():


} catch (Throwable t) {
    throw fatalFaultHandler.handleFault("Unexpected error in raft IO thread", t);
}


In production broker/controller wiring (SharedServer.scala), raftManagerFaultHandler is built from ProcessTerminatingFaultHandler. So this IllegalStateException does not just fail one request — it terminates the broker/controller process. That matches every reporter's symptom in this ticket: the node crashes, restarts, and (per the metrics quoted in the description) can come back up fenced with no leader known, requiring manual intervention.

This is reachable without any data corruption or misconfiguration

There are two call sites with no epoch-equality guard at all before maybeTransition: handleBeginQuorumEpochRequest and handleEndQuorumEpochRequest. Both validate the incoming request only via:


private Optional<Errors> validateVoterOnlyRequest(int remoteNodeId, int requestEpoch) {
    if (requestEpoch < quorum.epoch()) {
        return Optional.of(Errors.FENCED_LEADER_EPOCH);
    } else if (remoteNodeId < 0) {
        return Optional.of(Errors.INVALID_REQUEST);
    } else {
        return Optional.empty();
    }
}


Note this only rejects older epochs. A request where requestEpoch == quorum.epoch() and requestLeaderId == localId sails through this check and falls straight into maybeTransition. If the local node does not currently consider itself the leader of that epoch (e.g. it has already moved to ResignedState — where isLeader() is false even though quorum.epoch() has not been bumped yet), this is fatal.

This is a real, unprivileged distributed-systems hazard, not an edge case requiring a buggy/malicious peer: a duplicate or delayed BeginQuorumEpoch/EndQuorumEpoch request (completely normal under retries, slow networks, or partitions) that still names this node as leader of the current epoch is exactly the kind of message every Raft implementation has to tolerate as "stale" rather than treat as an invariant violation.

Notably, the Kafka maintainers are already aware this class of hazard exists — see the comment in pollResigned:


// The replica stays in resigned state for an election timeout period to allow end quorum requests
// to be processed, and to give other replicas a chance to become leader. When transitioning out
// of resigned state, the epoch must be increased to avoid FETCH responses with the leader
// being this replica, and to avoid this replica attempting to transition into follower state with
// itself as the leader.
transitionToUnattached(quorum.epoch() + 1, OptionalInt.empty());


That epoch bump specifically dodges the hazard for the Resigned → Unattached transition (any stale "leader=self" message at the old epoch is afterwards rejected gracefully as FENCED_LEADER_EPOCH, since the epoch moved on). But it only covers that one transition, and only once the election timeout has elapsed. For the window while still in ResignedState (same epoch, isLeader() == false already), and for any other path that reaches maybeTransition with a same-epoch self-referential leaderId, there is no equivalent protection — the fatal throw is still the only outcome.

I also want to flag: raft/src/test/java/org/apache/kafka/raft/KafkaRaftClientTest.java has no test coverage at all exercising this throw, which suggests it was never deliberately exercised as intended behavior — it reads as an unguarded internal assumption rather than an intentional fail-fast decision.

Proposed fix directions (looking for maintainer input before writing a patch)

# Make the inconsistency recoverable instead of fatal. Every other stale/unexpected condition nearby in this same code (UNKNOWN_LEADER_EPOCH, FENCED_LEADER_EPOCH, NOT_LEADER_OR_FOLLOWER, INVALID_VOTER_KEY) is handled by logging and returning/ignoring, not by throwing. Replacing the throw in maybeTransition with a logged warning + no-op (dropping the inconsistent request/response) would bring this in line with the rest of the method and remove the process-termination blast radius entirely.
# Tighten request validation before maybeTransition is reached. validateVoterOnlyRequest could additionally reject (with e.g. FENCED_LEADER_EPOCH or a new/existing error code) the specific case of requestEpoch == quorum.epoch() && requestLeaderId == localId && !quorum.isLeader(), so handleBeginQuorumEpochRequest/handleEndQuorumEpochRequest never call into maybeTransition with a self-inconsistent leader claim in the first place.
# Do both: (2) as defense-in-depth for the two request handlers specifically, plus (1) so the shared maybeTransition/hasConsistentLeader path is safe for any other caller (including the response path via maybeHandleCommonResponse) that might hit the same condition in the future.

I'd like maintainer input on which direction (or combination) is preferred, and whether there's a reason this was intentionally left fatal that I'm missing, before I put together a patch + tests (including a regression test reproducing the stale same-epoch self-leader request against a Resigned/Unattached node).

One more note on scope: a couple of the later comments on this ticket describe what looks like a separate, harder-to-pin-down contributing issue — the on-disk quorum-state epoch appearing to regress across a restart (e.g. "Epoch from quorum store file is 0, which is smaller than last written epoch 66"). That would make the crash reachable even more easily (a wider epoch gap re-triggers the same hasConsistentLeader failure), but I'm treating it as a distinct question from the crash-on-inconsistency design gap above, since I don't have a verified root cause for the epoch regression itself yet.

I run the test in a loop in a shell script like this:

#!/bin/bash
set -e

for i in \{1..100}; do
  echo ========================================================
  echo ======================== $i ============================
  echo ========================================================
  mvn surefire:test -Dtest=ChainedEmitStrategyTopologyTest3
done

I am attaching output of one such run, where first 5 iterations succeeded, but the 6th one failed

I poked a bit more, truncating the time to hour also works: Instant flowTime = baseTime.minus(3, ChronoUnit.HOURS).truncatedTo(ChronoUnit.HOURS);
I am not 100% sure why, but my impression so far is that because we aggregate every hour: {{{}TimeWindows.ofSizeAndGrace(Duration.ofHours(1), Duration.ofMinutes(10){}}}, so if we are start from not a round hour, it's possible that the messages we send in the test will not always end up in the windows we expect them to.
I still need to spend more time looking at the code to find the exact piece that's responsible for that, so my conclusion may not be 100% accurate, but the empirically achieved results kind of back up my assumptions.

