Thank you for the reproducer repo; it made this straightforward to narrow down. The punctuator plus caching combination was the key detail;

The mechanism: StreamTask#punctuate sets a dummy ProcessorRecordContext with partition -1, the cache captures it at delete time and replays it at flush, and `SubscriptionSendProcessorSupplier` copies it into the subscription wrapper.

Many mirror tests need to produce a bunch of records, but the helper method blocks until each record is completed. That is freaking slow for all tests...

We can refactor this while keeping the original timeout behaviour.


    protected void produceMessages(Producer<byte[], byte[]> producer, List<ProducerRecord<byte[], byte[]>> records) {
        Timer timer = Time.SYSTEM.timer(RECORD_PRODUCE_DURATION_MS);

        try {
            var futures = records.stream().map(producer::send).toList();
            for (var f : futures) {
                f.get(timer.remainingMs(), TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);
                timer.update();
            }
        } catch (ExecutionException | InterruptedException | TimeoutException e) {
            throw new RuntimeException(e);
        }
    }

