props.text:
Problem
In a streams group (KIP-1071), one process must never hold the same task twice. CurrentAssignmentBuilder says "a single process must never run the same task twice". The reconciler enforces this only when it grants a task: the isUnreleased*Task checks run on tasks pending assignment, and a task the member already holds is kept without any check.

A static member that rejoins under a new member ID bypasses that check:
# The rejoin is accepted once the old member has left with epoch -2. GroupMetadataManager#getOrMaybeCreateStaticStreamsGroupMember copies the old member, including its assigned tasks.
# The heartbeat then sets the process ID from the request. Nothing compares it with the old process ID.
# If the rejoin comes from a different process, the copied tasks move to that process. If another member there already holds one of them as a standby or warm-up task, that process now holds the task twice.

The new target assignment that the process ID change triggers does not remove the duplicate. The sticky assignor keeps the active task on the rejoined member. The standby on the sibling member is revoked, but it stays until that member acknowledges the revocation.

When it happens
 Kafka Streams with static membership: each stream thread uses group.instance.id "<id><threadIdx>". Member IDs are new on every restart, so a restarted thread always takes the rejoin path above.
* The process ID changes across a restart when the topology has no persistent stores (StateDirectory#initializeProcessId creates a random UUID on every start), or when the state directory is lost or the instance moves to another host.
* Sequence: an instance with two threads restarts under a new process ID B. Thread 2 rejoins first and is given the standby of task t, which is allowed because active t is still on the old process. Thread 1 rejoins and inherits active t. Process B now holds t both as an active task and as a standby task.

Impact
Both threads of the same instance are told to run t at the same time. This lasts until the next target assignment revokes the standby and the second thread acknowledges it: about one heartbeat interval, plus up to the assignment interval. From reading the client code:
* With persistent stores, the task-directory lock makes the new active task retry until the standby releases it, so t stalls.
* Without persistent stores, which is exactly the case where the process ID changes on every restart, the lock is a no-op. Both copies run, which duplicates memory and restore work.

Reproduction
The test below fails on trunk (dfd3dcab07) at the final assertion: process-B is both the active owner and a standby holder of s1_0.


package org.apache.kafka.coordinator.group;

import org.apache.kafka.common.Uuid;
import org.apache.kafka.common.message.StreamsGroupHeartbeatRequestData;
import org.apache.kafka.common.message.StreamsGroupHeartbeatRequestData.Subtopology;
import org.apache.kafka.common.message.StreamsGroupHeartbeatRequestData.TopicInfo;
import org.apache.kafka.common.message.StreamsGroupHeartbeatRequestData.Topology;
import org.apache.kafka.coordinator.common.runtime.CoordinatorMetadataImage;
import org.apache.kafka.coordinator.common.runtime.MetadataImageBuilder;
import org.apache.kafka.coordinator.group.streams.StreamsGroup;
import org.apache.kafka.coordinator.group.streams.StreamsGroupBuilder;
import org.apache.kafka.coordinator.group.streams.StreamsTopology;
import org.apache.kafka.coordinator.group.streams.TaskRole;
import org.apache.kafka.coordinator.group.streams.assignor.AssignmentConfigsImpl;
import org.apache.kafka.coordinator.group.streams.assignor.StickyTaskAssignor;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;

import static org.apache.kafka.common.requests.StreamsGroupHeartbeatRequest.JOIN_GROUP_MEMBER_EPOCH;
import static org.apache.kafka.common.requests.StreamsGroupHeartbeatRequest.LEAVE_GROUP_STATIC_MEMBER_EPOCH;
import static org.apache.kafka.coordinator.group.StreamsGroupTestUtil.mkResponseTasks;
import static org.apache.kafka.coordinator.group.StreamsGroupTestUtil.streamsGroupMemberBuilderWithDefaults;
import static org.apache.kafka.coordinator.group.Utils.computeGroupHash;
import static org.apache.kafka.coordinator.group.Utils.computeTopicHash;
import static org.apache.kafka.coordinator.group.streams.TaskAssignmentTestUtil.mkTasks;
import static org.apache.kafka.coordinator.group.streams.TaskAssignmentTestUtil.mkTasksTuple;
import static org.apache.kafka.coordinator.group.streams.TaskAssignmentTestUtil.mkTasksTupleWithCommonEpoch;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertFalse;

class StreamsStaticRejoinProcessExclusivityTest {

    @Test
    public void staticRejoinFromNewProcessMustNotPutATaskOnAProcessTwice() {
        // One Kafka Streams instance with two stream threads (static instance IDs app-1 and app-2) ran on process A,
        // with num.standby.replicas=1 and a single stateful task s1_0. Thread app-1 ran the active task. Both threads
        // left with epoch -2, and the instance restarted under a new process ID B (no persistent stores, or a wiped
        // state directory), so each thread rejoins under a new member ID.
        Topology topology = new Topology().setSubtopologies(List.of(new Subtopology()
            .setSubtopologyId("s1")
            .setSourceTopics(List.of("in"))
            .setStateChangelogTopics(List.of(new TopicInfo().setName("store-changelog")))));
        CoordinatorMetadataImage image = new MetadataImageBuilder()
            .addTopic(Uuid.randomUuid(), "in", 1)
            .addTopic(Uuid.randomUuid(), "store-changelog", 1)
            .buildCoordinatorMetadataImage();
        GroupMetadataManagerTestContext context = new GroupMetadataManagerTestContext.Builder()
            .withStreamsGroupTaskAssignors(List.of(new StickyTaskAssignor()))
            .withMetadataImage(image)
            .withConfig(GroupCoordinatorConfig.STREAMS_GROUP_NUM_STANDBY_REPLICAS_CONFIG, 1)
            .withStreamsGroup(new StreamsGroupBuilder("app", 10)
                .withMember(streamsGroupMemberBuilderWithDefaults("t1-old", "app-1")
                    .setMemberEpoch(LEAVE_GROUP_STATIC_MEMBER_EPOCH)
                    .setPreviousMemberEpoch(10)
                    .setProcessId("process-A")
                    .setAssignedTasks(mkTasksTupleWithCommonEpoch(TaskRole.ACTIVE, 0, mkTasks("s1", 0)))
                    .build())
                .withMember(streamsGroupMemberBuilderWithDefaults("t2-old", "app-2")
                    .setMemberEpoch(LEAVE_GROUP_STATIC_MEMBER_EPOCH)
                    .setPreviousMemberEpoch(10)
                    .setProcessId("process-A")
                    .build())
                .withTargetAssignment("t1-old", mkTasksTuple(TaskRole.ACTIVE, mkTasks("s1", 0)))
                .withTargetAssignment("t2-old", mkTasksTuple(TaskRole.ACTIVE))
                .withTopology(StreamsTopology.fromHeartbeatRequest(topology))
                .withTargetAssignmentEpoch(10)
                .withMetadataHash(computeGroupHash(Map.of(
                    "in", computeTopicHash("in", image),
                    "store-changelog", computeTopicHash("store-changelog", image))))
                .withValidatedTopologyEpoch(0)
                .withLastAssignmentConfigs(new AssignmentConfigsImpl(1, List.of())))
            .build();
        StreamsGroup group = context.groupMetadataManager.streamsGroup("app");

        // Thread app-2 rejoins first. The active task is still on process A, so B may take the standby.
        var thread2 = context.streamsGroupHeartbeat(rejoin("t2-new", "app-2", "process-B", topology));
        assertEquals(mkResponseTasks("s1", 0), thread2.response().data().standbyTasks());

        // Thread app-1 rejoins right after. It inherits its old member's assignment, active s1_0, and moves it to
        // process B, where thread app-2 already holds the standby of s1_0.
        var thread1 = context.streamsGroupHeartbeat(rejoin("t1-new", "app-1", "process-B", topology));
        assertEquals(mkResponseTasks("s1", 0), thread1.response().data().activeTasks());

        // Fails today: process B now holds s1_0 both as an active task (t1-new) and as a standby task (t2-new).
        assertFalse(
            "process-B".equals(group.currentActiveTaskProcessId("s1", 0))
                && group.currentStandbyTaskProcessIds("s1", 0).contains("process-B"),
            "process-B holds task s1_0 as an active task and as a standby task at the same time");
    }

    private static StreamsGroupHeartbeatRequestData rejoin(String memberId, String instanceId, String processId,
                                                           Topology topology) {
        return new StreamsGroupHeartbeatRequestData()
            .setGroupId("app")
            .setMemberId(memberId)
            .setMemberEpoch(JOIN_GROUP_MEMBER_EPOCH)
            .setInstanceId(instanceId)
            .setProcessId(processId)
            .setRebalanceTimeoutMs(1500)
            .setTopology(topology)
            .setActiveTasks(List.of())
            .setStandbyTasks(List.of())
            .setWarmupTasks(List.of());
    }
}


Possible fix
When a static member rejoins from a different process, do not keep copied tasks that the new process already holds in another role. For example, the reconciler could put those tasks back in pending assignment, so the existing exclusivity checks apply to them.

Problem
When a streams group heartbeat computes a new target assignment, \GroupMetadataManager#refineAssignment passes \group.members() to the refiner (GroupMetadataManager:4707 (https://github.com/apache/kafka/blob/f568b7b2c8802e6635b4d8df93ede88e55a2b84d/group-coordinator/src/main/java/org/apache/kafka/coordinator/group/GroupMetadataManager.java#L4707)). That map does not contain the member sending the heartbeat yet, because the heartbeat's records are replayed only after the handler returns. The target assignment the refiner gets, however, comes from \UpdatedMembersAndTargetAssignmentView, which already adds that member, or relabels a replaced static member to its new member ID (:4583-4595 (https://github.com/apache/kafka/blob/f568b7b2c8802e6635b4d8df93ede88e55a2b84d/group-coordinator/src/main/java/org/apache/kafka/coordinator/group/GroupMetadataManager.java#L4583-L4595)).

So the refiner treats a target owner that joins in this heartbeat as a member that has left the group, and its result is cached as the epoch's intermediate assignment.

Effects
With warm-up tasks enabled (the default), for stateful tasks:
# A static member that rejoins under a new member ID gets none of its tasks when the same heartbeat computes a new target, for example because it rejoins from a new process. The refiner keeps its active tasks on the old member ID, which the replacement removes. The member is told nothing, and no member runs those tasks until the next refinement step.
# *A new member gets no warm-up task.* A stateful task the target moves to it stays on its old owner without a warm-up, even when warm-up slots are free, until the next refinement step.
# A task can run \num.standby.replicas  1 standbys.* If the new member is the target standby holder of a task whose migration borrows an existing standby, its standby is not held back (AssignmentRefinerImpl:866-872 (https://github.com/apache/kafka/blob/f568b7b2c8802e6635b4d8df93ede88e55a2b84d/group-coordinator/src/main/java/org/apache/kafka/coordinator/group/streams/AssignmentRefinerImpl.java#L866-L872), :818-823 (https://github.com/apache/kafka/blob/f568b7b2c8802e6635b4d8df93ede88e55a2b84d/group-coordinator/src/main/java/org/apache/kafka/coordinator/group/streams/AssignmentRefinerImpl.java#L818-L823)). Once delivered it is kept, so the extra standby lasts until the migration completes.

The refiner's branches for a target owner missing from \members (AssignmentRefinerImpl:286-293 (https://github.com/apache/kafka/blob/f568b7b2c8802e6635b4d8df93ede88e55a2b84d/group-coordinator/src/main/java/org/apache/kafka/coordinator/group/streams/AssignmentRefinerImpl.java#L286-L293)) attribute that case to assignment offloading and member fencing. Neither produces it on trunk: streams assignor offloading is not wired up, and fencing removes the member's target assignment together with the member. So today these branches fire only for a member that joins in the same heartbeat.

Possible fix
Pass the view's members to the refiner, together with the view's target assignment.

Reproduction
The test below fails on trunk (\46dc4b51f7) with \expected: <[TaskIds(subtopologyId='s1', partitions=[0])]> but was: <[]>. Place it in \group-coordinator/src/test/java/org/apache/kafka/coordinator/group/. Effects 2 and 3 reproduce the same way, with a joining dynamic member.


