Thanks for the changes. 
However, I think this behaviour is introducing another case of starvation. The case isn't a very regular way of share fetch but it can occur and trunk was protected earlier, so seems like a regression. The case is when a share fetch hands its record budget to partitions in response order, so the first partition with enough data takes all of it. That order comes from two stacked mechanisms:

1. LRU order: the share session moves the partition it just served to the end of its list.
2. Epoch rotation: the broker then rotates the list by `sessionEpoch % numPartitions`.

The client advances the session epoch on every ShareFetch and every ShareAcknowledge. In a `poll(); commitSync();` loop the epoch therefore moves by 2 per fetch, so the rotation never changes. If the epoch is odd, rotation 1 puts the just-served partition first on every fetch with 2 partitions. That partition takes the whole budget each time, and the other starves for as long as the first has at least one fetch’s worth of records. With 4 partitions and odd parity, one partition is never served at all.

A single unpaired request is enough to make the epoch odd:

an empty fetch (for example, before the share partitions finish initializing, or during lock contention with another member),
or an extra acknowledge, which is how the test triggers it.

**Why trunk doesn’t starve:** the old fetch-offset cache on SharePartition happened to put the just-served partition last in the response, so the rotation never mattered. This PR reads every partition up front, so the response follows the rotated order and that accidental protection is gone.

Here is the test case you can use in `ShareConsumerFairnessTest.java` to prove it - 

```
    /**
     * In a poll and commit loop every share fetch is followed by one share acknowledge, and both advance the share
     * session epoch, hence the epoch moves by two between share fetches and the rotation of the partitions in the
     * share fetch does not change. A single unpaired request, such as a share fetch which returns no records or, as
     * in this test, the acknowledgements of one poll committed in two requests, flips the parity of the epoch. The
     * parity before the flip is not known upfront, hence both partitions must be served fairly in both phases.
     */
    @ClusterTest
    public void testPartitionsServedFairlyWhenShareSessionEpochParityChanges() throws Exception {
        String topic = "fairness-epoch-parity";
        String groupId = "fairness-epoch-parity-group";
        createTopic(topic, PARTITIONS, 1);
        produceRecords(topic);
        alterShareAutoOffsetReset(groupId, "earliest");

        Map<Integer, Integer> beforeParityChange;
        Map<Integer, Integer> afterParityChange;
        try (ShareConsumer<byte[], byte[]> consumer = createShareConsumer(groupId, Map.of(
            ConsumerConfig.MAX_POLL_RECORDS_CONFIG, String.valueOf(MAX_POLL_RECORDS),
            ConsumerConfig.SHARE_ACQUIRE_MODE_CONFIG, "record_limit",
            ConsumerConfig.SHARE_ACKNOWLEDGEMENT_MODE_CONFIG, "explicit"))) {
            consumer.subscribe(Set.of(topic));
            beforeParityChange = pollAndCommit(consumer);

            // Commit the acknowledgements of one poll in two requests, which flips the parity of the epoch.
            List<ConsumerRecord<byte[], byte[]>> records = pollRecords(consumer);
            int half = records.size() / 2;
            records.subList(0, half).forEach(consumer::acknowledge);
            consumer.commitSync();
            records.subList(half, records.size()).forEach(consumer::acknowledge);
            consumer.commitSync();

            afterParityChange = pollAndCommit(consumer);
        }

        // Each phase consumes far fewer records than the backlog of either partition, hence both partitions have
        // records throughout and each should get a meaningful share of the records of every phase.
        String recordsByPhase = "records by partition before the epoch parity changed: " + beforeParityChange
            + ", after: " + afterParityChange;
        for (Map<Integer, Integer> phase : List.of(beforeParityChange, afterParityChange)) {
            int phaseRecords = phase.values().stream().mapToInt(Integer::intValue).sum();
            for (int p = 0; p < PARTITIONS; p++) {
                assertTrue(phase.getOrDefault(p, 0) * 5 >= phaseRecords,
                    "Partition " + p + " was starved, " + recordsByPhase);
            }
        }
    }

    private void produceRecords(String topic) {
        try (Producer<byte[], byte[]> producer = createProducer()) {
            for (int i = 0; i < RECORDS_PER_PARTITION; i++) {
                for (int p = 0; p < PARTITIONS; p++) {
                    producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>(topic, p, null, null, "value".getBytes()));
                }
            }
            producer.flush();
        }
    }

    /**
     * Polls until {@link #POLLS_PER_PHASE} polls have returned records, and commits the acknowledgements of each poll
     * in one request.
     */
    private Map<Integer, Integer> pollAndCommit(ShareConsumer<byte[], byte[]> consumer) {
        Map<Integer, Integer> recordsByPartition = new TreeMap<>();
        for (int poll = 0; poll < POLLS_PER_PHASE; poll++) {
            for (ConsumerRecord<byte[], byte[]> record : pollRecords(consumer)) {
                recordsByPartition.merge(record.partition(), 1, Integer::sum);
                consumer.acknowledge(record);
            }
            consumer.commitSync();
        }
        return recordsByPartition;
    }

    /**
     * Polls until records are returned, the test timeout fails the test if no records are delivered.
     */
    private List<ConsumerRecord<byte[], byte[]>> pollRecords(ShareConsumer<byte[], byte[]> consumer) {
        List<ConsumerRecord<byte[], byte[]>> records = new ArrayList<>();
        while (records.isEmpty()) {
            consumer.poll(Duration.ofMillis(500)).forEach(records::add);
        }
        return records;
    }
```

