             Summary: Streams group: a static member that rejoins from a new process can put a task on that process twice
                 Key: KAFKA-21306
                 URL: https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/KAFKA-21306
             Project: Kafka
          Issue Type: Bug
          Components: streams
            Reporter: Gavin Wang
            Assignee: Gavin Wang


h3. Problem
In a streams group (KIP-1071), one process must never hold the same task twice. CurrentAssignmentBuilder says "a single process must never run the same task twice". The reconciler enforces this only when it grants a task: the isUnreleased*Task checks run on tasks pending assignment, and a task the member already holds is kept without any check.

A static member that rejoins under a new member ID bypasses that check:
# The rejoin is accepted once the old member has left with epoch -2. GroupMetadataManager#getOrMaybeCreateStaticStreamsGroupMember copies the old member, including its assigned tasks.
# The heartbeat then sets the process ID from the request. Nothing compares it with the old process ID.
# If the rejoin comes from a different process, the copied tasks move to that process. If another member there already holds one of them as a standby or warm-up task, that process now holds the task twice.

The new target assignment that the process ID change triggers does not remove the duplicate. The sticky assignor keeps the active task on the rejoined member. The standby on the sibling member is revoked, but it stays until that member acknowledges the revocation.

h3. When it happens
* Kafka Streams with static membership: each stream thread uses group.instance.id "<id>-<threadIdx>". Member IDs are new on every restart, so a restarted thread always takes the rejoin path above.
* The process ID changes across a restart when the topology has no persistent stores (StateDirectory#initializeProcessId creates a random UUID on every start), or when the state directory is lost or the instance moves to another host.
* Sequence: an instance with two threads restarts under a new process ID B. Thread 2 rejoins first and is given the standby of task t, which is allowed because active t is still on the old process. Thread 1 rejoins and inherits active t. Process B now holds t both as an active task and as a standby task.

h3. Impact
Both threads of the same instance are told to run t at the same time. This lasts until the next target assignment revokes the standby and the second thread acknowledges it: about one heartbeat interval, plus up to the assignment interval. From reading the client code:
* With persistent stores, the task-directory lock makes the new active task retry until the standby releases it, so t stalls.
* Without persistent stores, which is exactly the case where the process ID changes on every restart, the lock is a no-op. Both copies run, which duplicates memory and restore work.

h3. Reproduction
The test below fails on trunk (dfd3dcab07) at the final assertion: process-B is both the active owner and a standby holder of s1_0.

