             Summary: Streams group: the assignment refiner does not see a member that joins in the same heartbeat
                 Key: KAFKA-21321
                 URL: https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/KAFKA-21321
             Project: Kafka
          Issue Type: Bug
          Components: group-coordinator, streams
    Affects Versions: 4.5.0
            Reporter: Gavin Wang


h3. Problem
When a streams group heartbeat computes a new target assignment, \{{GroupMetadataManager#refineAssignment}} passes \{{group.members()}} to the refiner ([GroupMetadataManager:4707|https://github.com/apache/kafka/blob/f568b7b2c8802e6635b4d8df93ede88e55a2b84d/group-coordinator/src/main/java/org/apache/kafka/coordinator/group/GroupMetadataManager.java#L4707]). That map does not contain the member sending the heartbeat yet, because the heartbeat's records are replayed only after the handler returns. The target assignment the refiner gets, however, comes from \{{UpdatedMembersAndTargetAssignmentView}}, which already adds that member, or relabels a replaced static member to its new member ID ([:4583-4595|https://github.com/apache/kafka/blob/f568b7b2c8802e6635b4d8df93ede88e55a2b84d/group-coordinator/src/main/java/org/apache/kafka/coordinator/group/GroupMetadataManager.java#L4583-L4595]).

So the refiner treats a target owner that joins in this heartbeat as a member that has left the group, and its result is cached as the epoch's intermediate assignment.

h3. Effects
With warm-up tasks enabled (the default), for stateful tasks:
# *A static member that rejoins under a new member ID gets none of its tasks* when the same heartbeat computes a new target, for example because it rejoins from a new process. The refiner keeps its active tasks on the old member ID, which the replacement removes. The member is told nothing, and no member runs those tasks until the next refinement step.
# *A new member gets no warm-up task.* A stateful task the target moves to it stays on its old owner without a warm-up, even when warm-up slots are free, until the next refinement step.
# *A task can run \{{num.standby.replicas + 1}} standbys.* If the new member is the target standby holder of a task whose migration borrows an existing standby, its standby is not held back ([AssignmentRefinerImpl:866-872|https://github.com/apache/kafka/blob/f568b7b2c8802e6635b4d8df93ede88e55a2b84d/group-coordinator/src/main/java/org/apache/kafka/coordinator/group/streams/AssignmentRefinerImpl.java#L866-L872], [:818-823|https://github.com/apache/kafka/blob/f568b7b2c8802e6635b4d8df93ede88e55a2b84d/group-coordinator/src/main/java/org/apache/kafka/coordinator/group/streams/AssignmentRefinerImpl.java#L818-L823]). Once delivered it is kept, so the extra standby lasts until the migration completes.

The refiner's branches for a target owner missing from \{{members}} ([AssignmentRefinerImpl:286-293|https://github.com/apache/kafka/blob/f568b7b2c8802e6635b4d8df93ede88e55a2b84d/group-coordinator/src/main/java/org/apache/kafka/coordinator/group/streams/AssignmentRefinerImpl.java#L286-L293]) attribute that case to assignment offloading and member fencing. Neither produces it on trunk: streams assignor offloading is not wired up, and fencing removes the member's target assignment together with the member. So today these branches fire only for a member that joins in the same heartbeat.

h3. Possible fix
Pass the view's members to the refiner, together with the view's target assignment.

h3. Reproduction
The test below fails on trunk (\{{46dc4b51f7}}) with \{{expected: <[TaskIds(subtopologyId='s1', partitions=[0])]> but was: <[]>}}. Place it in \{{group-coordinator/src/test/java/org/apache/kafka/coordinator/group/}}. Effects 2 and 3 reproduce the same way, with a joining dynamic member.

