The encoded long is a signed count of microseconds since the Unix epoch; negative values represent instants before 1970. Because Instant stores epochSecond (which may be negative) with nanoOfSecond always in [0, 999_999_999], negative instants round-trip exactly. Decoding uses floor division/modulo; encoding is overflow-checked:
 static long fromLogical(Schema schema, Instant value) {
     // schema name check ...
    return Math.addExact(
         Math.multiplyExact(value.getEpochSecond(), 1_000_000L),
         value.getNano() / 1_000L);   // sub-microsecond nanoseconds truncated
 }
 static Instant toLogical(Schema schema, long micros) {
     // schema name check ...
     return Instant.ofEpochSecond(
         Math.floorDiv(micros, 1_000_000L),
         Math.floorMod(micros, 1_000_000L) * 1_000L);
 }
 Example: 1969-12-31T23:59:59.999999Z (epochSecond = -1, nano = 999_999_000) encodes to -1 and decodes back to the same instant.

